# Arena — environment variables
# Copy to .env.local for local dev, or `vercel env pull .env.local` to pull
# real values already configured in the Vercel dashboard. Never commit a real
# value. Add a var here in the same change that starts requiring it —
# see docs/DEVELOPMENT.md.

# --- Neon Postgres (Marketplace) ---
# Provisioned via Vercel dashboard → Storage → Neon Postgres → Connect to
# Project. Do not hand-create this — the Marketplace integration wires it in.
DATABASE_URL=

# --- Vercel Blob ---
# Provisioned via Vercel dashboard → Storage → Blob → Create Store.
BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=

# --- Edge Config ---
# Provisioned via Vercel dashboard → Storage → Edge Config → Create Store.
EDGE_CONFIG=

# --- Edge Config write access (M3, ops/) ---
# `EDGE_CONFIG` above is read-only by design. Edge Config is a team-level
# resource, not a project one, so writing to it needs a full Vercel API
# token — there's no narrower option (confirmed against the real API while
# building this, not assumed). Generate one at vercel.com → Account Settings
# → Tokens, named clearly (e.g. "arena-edge-config-writer") so it's easy to
# find and revoke independently of any personal login token. Optional at
# runtime: the Cron rotation route degrades gracefully without it (still
# selects the challenge and starts the Workflow, just can't flip the pointer
# the public site reads) rather than failing the whole rotation.
EDGE_CONFIG_WRITE_TOKEN=

# --- AI Gateway ---
# No separate API key required for Vercel's own AI Gateway when running on
# Vercel infra (auth is via the platform's own OIDC token). If running
# `vercel dev` and it's not picking this up automatically, check current
# AI Gateway docs — this mechanism has moved before.
AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY=

# --- Connect (beta) ---
# GitHub's connector is registered in the Vercel dashboard under Connect —
# short-lived, scoped tokens requested at call time, no standing PAT here.
# Confirmed against the live product (M3): Connect's connector types are
# api-key, github, linear, oauth, photon, salesforce, slack, snowflake —
# Discord isn't one of them, so the digest below uses a different mechanism.
# If you find yourself about to add a standing GitHub token, stop and re-read
# docs/SECURITY.md.
CONNECT_PROJECT_ID=

# --- Discord digest (M3, ops/) ---
# A Discord Incoming Webhook, not Connect — see the CONNECT_PROJECT_ID note
# above for why. Create one on the target server: Server Settings →
# Integrations → Webhooks → New Webhook, then copy its URL. Scoped to exactly
# one channel and revocable from Discord's own side, which is most of what
# docs/SECURITY.md's "no standing broad credential" concern is about, even
# though it's a plain env var rather than a Connect-issued token. Optional at
# runtime — a missing digest doesn't undo an already-completed, already-
# scored race, so this fails loud (logged) rather than failing the Workflow.
DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL=

# --- Internal/operator auth ---
# Shared secret for /api/internal/* routes (docs/API.md). Generate a real
# random value, never reuse across environments.
INTERNAL_TOKEN=

# --- Cron verification ---
# Sent automatically by Vercel Cron as `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET`
# once this is set as a project env var — confirmed against current Vercel
# Cron docs while building the M3 rotation route, not assumed. Generate a
# real random value.
CRON_SECRET=

# --- Flags (M3, ops/) ---
# Authenticates inbound requests to app/.well-known/vercel/flags (Flags
# Explorer's discovery endpoint) — separate from the outbound auth
# `vercelAdapter()` uses to read/write flag values, which goes through this
# app's own Vercel OIDC token and needs no env var of its own. Generate a
# real random value.
FLAGS_SECRET=
